#N canvas 20 20 3800 2750 12;
#X text 20 20 u_net -- the phone status link \, and the SOLE OWNER of the fourth display surface. It consumes disp exactly as g_oled \, g_led and g_grid do \, and forwards what a phone can usefully show over UDP OSC. It writes no global name except err \, and nothing anywhere else may learn that a phone exists., f 58;
#X text 700 20 creation arg 1: the phone's host \, e.g. 192.168.1.5. creation arg 2: its OSC receive port -- 8000 for PdParty. NEVER 9000 \, which is PdParty's WebDAV server \, and never 4001-4003 \, which belong to mother. inlets: none. outlets: none., f 58;
#X text 1400 20 STATE \, NEVER EVENTS. Every message carries the whole current value \, so a dropped datagram is corrected by the next one rather than lost forever. That is what makes UDP acceptable here -- and it is why the alert below is held and repeated rather than sent once., f 58;
#X text 20 220 FIRE AND FORGET -- THE ORGANELLE NEVER WAITS. There is no handshake \, no retry on a datagram and nothing blocking. Phone off \, phone crashed \, wifi gone: the instrument plays identically. The only thing that ever retries is the CONNECTION \, for the reason in the watchdog block below., f 58;
#X text 700 220 THE RESERVED SELECTORS ARE MATCHED AND LEFT UNCONNECTED \, which is the whole reason the route box lists them. Everything g_oled does not recognise is a parameter by definition \, so without a branch here in-l \, grid and led would all fall out of the reject and reach the phone as nonsense parameters. Same shape as g_oled's own route \, same argument order \, so the two can be read side by side., f 58;
#X text 1400 220 THE LEVEL METERS AND THE GRID ARE DELIBERATELY DROPPED. in-l and in-r change continuously whenever there is audio -- measured at Phase 7 Step 0 as the ENTIRE resting content of the disp bus -- and would spend the whole rate budget on something the phone does not draw. grid is the Launchpad's own vocabulary and means nothing here., f 58;
#X obj 20 520 r disp;
#X obj 20 570 route in-l in-r status modal modal-off alert led grid;
#X obj 560 660 list append;
#X obj 560 1010 oscformat cutit status;
#X obj 560 1060 s \$0-osc;
#X text 300 1150 status IS RATE LIMITED TOO \, and that is not obvious: u_tempo writes the BPM into the footer and knob 1 is master tempo \, so ONE knob sweep put 201 status messages on the bus in a second when it was measured at Step 0. Untreated it costs as much as every parameter put together. One slot is enough \, because there is only ever one status., f 44;
#X obj 1050 660 list append;
#X obj 1050 710 t l b;
#X obj 1270 760 f;
#X obj 1410 760 + 1;
#X obj 1270 810 t f f;
#X obj 1050 870 list prepend;
#X obj 1050 920 s \$0-alert;
#X text 1050 980 AN ALERT IS AN EVENT \, AND UDP CANNOT CARRY EVENTS. Sent once it can be dropped and lost for the rest of the session. So it is turned into STATE: the last alert and a monotonic count are held below and repeated on every heartbeat \, which costs two datagrams a second and makes a dropped one self-correcting. The count is bumped from the RIGHT outlet of the trigger \, so it is already updated by the time the list reaches list prepend., f 58;
#X obj 1900 660 list append;
#X obj 1900 710 list append -;
#X obj 1900 760 list split 3;
#X obj 2800 1780 oscformat cutit param;
#X obj 2800 1830 s \$0-osc;
#X text 2350 920 THE UNIT IS MADE MANDATORY BY CONSTRUCTION. disp carries name value and an OPTIONAL unit \, and list split 3 on exactly three atoms never fires its right outlet -- C-7 \, the one that makes chop-size 43 % followed by grain 12 draw as grain 12 %. Appending a dash BEFORE the split means there is always a third atom \, so the field is written on every message instead of on some. A message that already had a unit loses the spare dash out the middle outlet., f 58;
#X obj 20 1240 loadbang;
#X obj 20 1290 del 1600;
#X obj 20 1340 metro 500;
#X obj 20 1390 t b b;
#X obj 20 1450 f;
#X obj 160 1450 + 1;
#X obj 20 1570 oscformat cutit hb;
#X obj 20 1620 s \$0-osc;
#X obj 330 1450 list append 0 none - -;
#X obj 330 1500 oscformat cutit alert;
#X obj 330 1550 s \$0-osc;
#X obj 330 1390 r \$0-alert;
#X text 20 2450 THE HEARTBEAT IS THE ONLY THING SEPARATING IDLE FROM DEAD \, so it keeps flowing when nothing else is happening. The phone restarts a 1500 ms timer on every datagram it receives \; the heartbeat is 500 ms \, so the last one is at most half a second old when the link drops and NO-LINK appears between 1.0 and 1.5 s later. THE DETECTOR HAS TO LIVE ON THE PHONE -- UDP is fire and forget and there is no reply \, so the Organelle cannot know the phone is gone. Same lesson as the Launchpad watchdog: the platform that can detect the loss is the one that has to., f 70;
#X obj 700 1240 loadbang;
#X obj 700 1290 del 1500;
#X obj 700 1750 symbol;
#X obj 700 1800 list append \$2;
#X obj 700 1850 list prepend connect;
#X obj 700 1900 list trim;
#X obj 700 1950 s \$0-tonet;
#X obj 1150 1240 loadbang;
#X obj 1150 1290 del 3000;
#X obj 1150 1340 metro 5000;
#X obj 1150 1390 spigot 1;
#X obj 1350 1340 r \$0-down;
#X text 2400 2300 THE \$1 TRAP IS WHY THIS IS FOUR OBJECT BOXES AND NOT ONE MESSAGE BOX. In an OBJECT box \$1 is a creation argument resolved once at load \; in a MESSAGE box it is the first element of the incoming message. A message box typed connect \$1 \$2 would therefore send the host and port of whatever banged it \, which is nothing at all. f \$2 and list prepend \$1 are both object boxes \, so both really do carry the creation args., f 58;
#X text 1150 1460 THE RECONNECT IS LOAD-BEARING \, NOT A NICETY \, and Step 0 is what proved it. A UDP connect to a host that is up with nothing listening SUCCEEDS \, survives exactly one datagram \, and is then torn down by the ICMP port-unreachable that comes back -- after which every send is discarded in SILENCE. That is the ordinary case of a phone on wifi with PdParty closed \, and without this the link would stay dead for the whole session with nothing to show for it. The spigot starts OPEN so a connect that never came up at all still retries \, and the first success closes it -- reconnecting an already-open socket prints an error every time \, which was measured too. The UDP-connect behaviour is a Trap on ref/device/phone.md., f 70;
#X obj 20 1900 r \$0-osc;
#X obj 20 1950 list prepend send;
#X obj 20 2000 list trim;
#X obj 20 2070 netsend -u -b;
#X obj 420 2010 r \$0-tonet;
#X obj 20 2130 change -1;
#X obj 20 2180 == 0;
#X obj 20 2230 s \$0-down;
#X text 20 2290 THE -1 IS LOAD-BEARING \, as it is in u_level and m_organelle: a bare change starts life holding 0 and would swallow the first real 0 -- which here is the disconnection the watchdog exists to notice., f 44;
#X obj 800 2130 r \$0-down;
#X obj 800 2180 select 1;
#X obj 800 2230 spigot 1;
#X obj 800 2280 t b b;
#X msg 1010 2330 warn u_net net-link-down;
#X obj 1010 2380 s err;
#X msg 800 2330 0;
#X obj 800 2380 s \$0-warngate;
#X obj 1080 2230 r \$0-warngate;
#X text 1400 2130 WARNED ONCE PER LOAD \, AND THE SPIGOT IS WHAT MAKES THAT TRUE. u_err writes its persistent log through a shell fork \, and Phase 4's rule is one fork per load and never one per event -- so a phone that is simply switched off must not raise a warning every five seconds for the rest of the set. The trigger sends the warning from its RIGHT outlet and closes the gate from its left \, so the gate is still open when the message goes. What actually reports the link is the phone itself \, which says NO-LINK \; this is only a breadcrumb for the log., f 70;
#X obj 2100 1900 loadbang;
#X obj 2100 1950 del 2000;
#X obj 2100 2000 f \$2;
#X obj 2100 2050 list prepend \$1;
#X obj 2100 2100 print u_net-target;
#X text 2100 2160 BEHIND del 2000 BECAUSE tools/deploy.sh GATES ON OUTPUT. The syntax check loads the patch and quits immediately \, so a print that fires at loadbang would break the deploy \, while a delayed one still reaches the by-hand console., f 44;
#X obj 160 1500 t f f;
#X obj 1900 1150 t l l;
#X obj 2100 1200 list split 1;
#X obj 2100 1250 text search \$0-pending 0;
#X obj 2100 1300 moses 0;
#X obj 2350 1350 t b;
#X obj 2350 1400 text size \$0-pending;
#X obj 1900 1500 text set \$0-pending;
#X obj 3300 1560 text define \$0-pending;
#X obj 1600 900 loadbang;
#X obj 1600 950 del 1700;
#X obj 1600 1000 metro 50;
#X obj 1600 1050 t b b;
#X obj 2800 1300 text size \$0-pending;
#X obj 2800 1350 t b f b;
#X msg 3100 1300 0;
#X obj 2800 1450 until;
#X obj 2800 1500 f;
#X obj 2800 1550 t f f;
#X obj 3020 1600 + 1;
#X obj 2800 1660 text get \$0-pending;
#X msg 3300 1480 clear;
#X obj 560 710 t l b;
#X msg 780 760 1;
#X obj 780 810 s \$0-sdirty;
#X obj 560 760 s \$0-sval;
#X obj 560 960 list append -;
#X obj 780 900 r \$0-sval;
#X obj 300 880 f;
#X obj 440 840 r \$0-sdirty;
#X obj 300 930 select 1;
#X obj 300 980 t b b;
#X msg 300 1030 0;
#X obj 300 1080 s \$0-sdirty;
#X obj 1600 1110 s \$0-tick-p;
#X obj 1780 1110 s \$0-tick-s;
#X obj 2800 1240 r \$0-tick-p;
#X obj 300 830 r \$0-tick-s;
#X text 3300 1660 PRESENCE IN THE TEXT IS THE DIRTY BIT \, which is what makes this smaller than g_oled's store: a row exists only because it is waiting to be sent \, so there is no dirty column to walk and no global flag to keep in step. The flush walks every row \, sends it \, and clears the whole text -- and because until is bounded by text size \, an idle tick runs the loop ZERO times and costs one message. Coalescing falls out of it: a name written twice between two ticks overwrites its own row \, and whatever was written last is what leaves. That is the guaranteed trailing edge \, per name rather than per bus., f 58;
#X text 2350 1560 ONE moses DECIDES UPDATE-OR-APPEND \, exactly as it does in g_oled. text search returns the row for a name already waiting and -1 for one it has not seen \; a hit is the row to overwrite \, and a miss falls through to the current size \, which text set treats as an append. THE t b IS NOT OPTIONAL: moses's left outlet emits the VALUE it did not match -- the -1 -- and text size passes a float straight through \, so without it -1 arrives at text set as a line number and errors. Second time that shape has bitten this repo., f 58;
#X text 2350 1180 50 ms IS THE WHOLE RATE LIMIT \, and it is the only number here that a person would want to change. Faster costs datagrams \; slower makes a fader feel laggy on the phone., f 44;
#X obj 1400 1700 loadbang;
#X obj 1400 1750 del 1800;
#X obj 1400 1800 metro 2000;
#X obj 1400 1850 t b b;
#X obj 1400 1900 s \$0-slow-p;
#X obj 1620 1900 s \$0-slow-s;
#X obj 2800 1710 t l l;
#X obj 3150 1650 r \$0-slow-p;
#X obj 3150 1700 list append;
#X obj 860 1010 r \$0-slow-s;
#X text 1400 1960 THE SLOW REPEAT \, FOR A PHONE THAT ARRIVES LATE. Parameters and status are sent only when they CHANGE \, so a scene opened mid-session showed empty rows until somebody moved something -- found on the device \, item 121. Every two seconds the last parameter and the last status are re-sent \, which is the same trick the alert already uses and costs about one datagram a second. THE ALERT NEVER HAD THIS PROBLEM because it is repeated on every heartbeat \, and the OLED never had it because it redraws held state every frame. u_net was the only surface where a late viewer saw nothing., f 70;
#X text 2800 1900 BANGING AN EMPTY STORE IS SAFE and needs no has-it-ever-fired flag. Measured: it sends a valid /cutit/param carrying no arguments \, and on the phone that reaches list split 1's TOO-SHORT outlet \, so neither the name nor the value is touched and nothing errors. The status store carries a dash as its creation argument for the same reason -- its branch has no such guard \, so it gets a real symbol to draw instead., f 58;
#X obj 700 1340 t b b;
#X obj 700 1390 symbol \$1;
#X obj 700 1440 list prepend phone-ip.sh;
#X obj 700 1490 list prepend sh;
#X obj 700 1540 list trim;
#X obj 700 1590 shell;
#X obj 700 1640 t a a;
#X msg 200 1690 stop;
#X obj 400 1740 del 700;
#X obj 400 1790 symbol \$1;
#X text 1400 2300 u_net IS NOT TOLD WHERE THE PHONE IS -- IT ASKS. phone-ip.sh reads the dnsmasq lease off the Organelle's OWN access point \, where the Organelle handed the address out itself and therefore already knows it \, and falls back to the creation argument anywhere else. So the same patch works on the house wifi and on the stage network with no edit \, which a literal address cannot do: it is right on exactly one network and dead on the others., f 58;
#X text 2400 2500 THE del 700 IS THE FALLBACK \, AND IT IS WHAT KEEPS THE MAC WORKING. shell is an external that exists only on the Organelle -- mac-stubs/shell.pd swallows everything and answers nothing -- so off-device the script never replies and this timer fires instead \, connecting to the creation argument exactly as before. It also covers the script failing or hanging on the device. A reply arrives first and stops it., f 58;
#X text 1150 2560 THE RETRY STARTS AT 3000 ms AND THAT NUMBER IS LOAD-BEARING. metro fires the INSTANT it is started \, so a retry armed before the address had been resolved banged an empty symbol store and sent connect with no host -- one bad host? on the console at every single boot. The address is resolved by 1550 ms on the device and by 2200 on the Mac \, where the del 700 fallback covers a shell that never answers \, so 3000 is after both. By then a successful connect has already closed the spigot \, so the metro's own first fire is blocked and costs nothing. FOUND BY RUNNING THE BENCH ON THE MAC \, which is the step this phase had skipped. Both resolution timings are on ref/device/phone.md., f 70;
